A||||17||1FST2M6||Polys not working...||||5||
Z||000000||1FST2M6||01-17-2000||11:16 AM||||I have poly bushings in the rear A-arms of my 86 SE V6 4 spd. I also have a 4 puck UNsprung clutch. when i let off the clutch the rear tires lunge forward and the car doesn't, as much as the rear suspension. We noticed it on the dyno. This unwanted movement is giving me some terrible wheel hop issues. It did it with the RAM clutch i had too. What i'm getting at is does anyone make a set of DELRIN, or aluminum or even a roller bearing replacement to remedy my problem? (I have Konis and Eibach springs)||||reg||5
Z||000001||batboy||01-17-2000||03:09 PM||||Are you sure it's not bad cradle bushings?||||reg||1
Z||000002||1FST2M6||01-17-2000||03:32 PM||||I don't think they can go bad.. thier aluminum. from Goza Machine. Just like the ones Christ West makes. But the wheel moves A LOT! fwd and back you can't move them with your hand or a long bar but with the clutch engagement OH YEAH! I have a Fiero Store dog bone with Delrin bushings instead of poly (from Paeco in birmingham, Al.) ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Patrick||01-17-2000||03:46 PM||||<BR>This is a bit over my head, but is there any chance that because numerous "rubber" bushings have been replaced with stiffer materials, that the few flexible bushings left are being forced to compress more than they originally would? So instead of every bushing allowing a little bit of movement, a few bushings are allowing a lot of movement? And maybe this is happening in an area that is resulting in the exhibited symptoms? The unsprung clutch would only make this situation worse.<P>Just a wild guess.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||DRH||01-17-2000||03:50 PM||||The site below is someone that did a 3800 conversion. Read the Rear Suspension paragraph and see if it might be your problem???<BR> <A HREF="http://members.home.net/gene-down/problems.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://members.home.net/gene-down/problems.htm</A> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||cobra427||01-18-2000||02:24 AM||||why are you using that 4 puck clutch with no springs? I have never seen one work properly.<BR>VW used to sell them for their modified engines.IMHO, they are a piece of junk. They have a tendency to chatter and do all sorts of weird things. I'll bet if you were to change that clutch disc, you'll see a big difference. A clutch needs to have a transition factor incorporated into it hence the springs in the disc. It appears that you have little there to accomplish this.(A very ridgid system) If my memory serves me correctly, thos types of disc's were designed for a heavy duty type racing.Drags, sandrails and the like.  Just a thought.<p>[This message has been edited by cobra427 (edited 01-18-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||1FST2M6||01-18-2000||12:58 PM||||The guys at the speed shop read the site you refered me too and sure 'nuff... its a bit of the oval bushing issue and over stiffing of the rest of the car and that the only place left to move... Chris West (West Coast Fieros) Is making me a set of Roller bearing replacement bushing/bearings...!!!  As for the why i'm running a 4 puck. I've had 5 clutches since sept. 1st of 98 when i bought the car with 83k miles (94+K now). it had the factory unit then, After the nitrous kit the springs in the clutch would either compress, brake and lodge in between the flywheel or the disc would snap in 3 or four pieces or Just a decent launch would snap the the center hub out of a RAM I had. And the Centerforce i had would slip really bad under nitrous, when i was at the tree on the strip, she was fine once the rpms were up, (the higher the rpms the more it holds) but launching at 1300 she just glazed over and would barely get me home from the track. The tranny can take a hell of a beating but the clutches were never ment to have 300+lb-ft of torque applied to them. And drag racing and Driving to work is all i do with the car. (13.6@103 Atl international Dragway July 30, 99) I'm suprised that all the V8 guys don't have clutch issues. The 4 puck does chatter a bit but not to bad.. Its a daily driver. The center hub is T6/a01 Extruded Aluminum and the plate a shiney black metal plate with Cinder ceramic/copper metalic pucks. (don't know what that means i'm reading the recipt) The biggest thing was when the nitrous was on she would just kill herself after a couple weeks/bottles. I've put more miles on this clutch than on all the others combined! and she still holds up perfect. I can chirp the tires going into 4th at 80+ with out nitrous! Not really that impressive if youve ever driven a car with a clutch like this but all the onlookers are impressed. I sent a pic of the clutch to Batboy. I've replaced the Flywheel twice. do to the spring issues we had cause you can't turn a BIG groove out of the flywheel.. thats what the local machine guys shop say. <BR><p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 01-18-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||01-18-2000||10:43 PM||||Somethings gotta give folks.......<P>You replaced the cradle with AL.<P>You replaced the dogbone with DuPont Delrin.<P>You put polly in the A arms.<P>You focused the entire surge energy in the A arms. Yup. The A arms Move. Big suprise. You get the same problem in a FWD car and even in a 'Vette.<P>You can make replacement arms with nylon lined sphericals.<P>you can rework the curent arm to accept nylon lined spheres.<P>you can spin some Delrin or brass to replace the polly A arm set. you also hace to make a bearing grade sleave for them to pivot on.<P>you can get a set of greasable needles from Moog as I recall someone saying recently....||||reg||1
Z||000008||Patrick||01-18-2000||10:53 PM||||<BR>Hey, my "wild guess" is lookin' pretty good... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000009||lowCG||01-18-2000||10:54 PM||||No one has mentioned motor/tranny mounts,the cradle mounts are so far apart that the mechanical advantage would be minimal where stress is higher closer to the center of the rotating parts.I've always wanted to try having everything solid mounted to the subframe,then leaving the rubber where the subframe mounts to the car for a little relief from the harshness normally felt by solid suspension/engine mounts.Can't imagine using a puck clutch on the street though,I mean,what do you do when a cop pulls up next to you at a stoplight and it turns green?||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||01-19-2000||07:49 PM||||In this case, and most other involving rear alignment changes. The engine mounts are a non issue. Movement in the motor/tranny set is not able to affect the wheels. Under all conditions but 2 the engine movment is eliminated in the halfshafts.<P>The only time engine torque movement affects things is when the cradle moves around. Such as in a manual tranny car when the clutch grabs or you let out of the gas suddenly. those types of sudden changes are transmitted unevenly to the cradle because the upper dogbone is not centered. The result is that the motor load pushes or pulls the right side of the cradle more than the left. If the dogbone went to the cradle instead of the body then this would not happen.<P>The problem here is a tourqe reaction, but it's not engine movement causing it. It's simple physics. when the wheel turns one way the car is trying to turn the other. Of course it can't so something else has to move, or break. End result is car can't be rotated counter to the wheels, the only thing that moves is the A arm.<P>With as rigid as this car is becoming I'd be worried about breaking things. I'd have left the polly bushings in the ultimate dog bone for sure. You need some give in the bone. Other wise you could find cracks in either or both brackets soon.||||reg||1
Z||000011||lowCG||01-20-2000||07:46 PM||||So the wheels are squatting forward?<BR>Maybe upper strut mounts?<BR>I was imagining a forward rotation,not a forward thrust.||||reg||1
Z||000012||WKDFIRO||01-20-2000||07:54 PM||||If there is that much torque going through the A-arm as you say there is....<P>DON'T FORGET TO REINFORCE THE A-ARMS!<P>Welding plates on the bottom of the arms will get rid of the flex the A-arms might be going through. Don't know if that is the problem you are describing but its one of those things you don't want to over look.<P>As for the V8s, Fiero Motorsports used a McLeod Multi-disc clutch, apparently the same used in Busch National racing, and the customer was very satisfied. That particular car was driven all the way to Michigan by the new owner with out a problem. Perhaps this is a consideration for your problems?<p>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 01-20-2000).]||||reg||4
Z||000013||geyser||01-21-2000||02:30 PM||||1FST2M6, Moroso offers a hardened steel set of rear control arm bushings. They are made by Herb Adams. Part # 87045. Let me know how they work if you try them.  Patrick, you are a genius. WKD, I was contemplating reinforcing the A arms as you describe, have you done it?<p>[This message has been edited by geyser (edited 01-22-2000).]||||reg||1
